(EXP-83: a radix cast reads the whole text as digits of its base. A text
 with a character outside the base is not a number in that base: the error
 flag is raised and a `Set` leaves its destination as it was. The manual's
 own example is `"12g5" as a hex number`; the texts are variables here
 because a literal that is not a number is refused at compile time.
 Up to 0.4.15 the cast stopped at the first invalid character instead, and
 this probe printed 18, 12, 5 and 15.
 Ran on vox main dfdfc7f: vox EXP-83.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   hex: flag raised, still 7
   base 10: flag raised, still 7
   binary: flag raised, still 7
   octal: flag raised, still 7
   prefixed: 31
)
a number called kept is 7.
a text called sixteens is "12g5".
Set kept to sixteens as a hex number.
On error Print "hex: flag raised, still {kept}".
a text called tens is "12xy".
Set kept to tens as a number.
On error Print "base 10: flag raised, still {kept}".
a text called twos is "1012".
Set kept to twos as a binary number.
On error Print "binary: flag raised, still {kept}".
a text called eights is "1789".
Set kept to eights as an octal number.
On error Print "octal: flag raised, still {kept}".
a text called prefixed is "0x1F".
Set kept to prefixed as a hex number.
On error Print "prefixed: flag raised".
Print "prefixed: {kept}".
